.SH NAME
.PP
\f[B]srs\-server\f[R] \- an open\-source video server, supports
RTMP/WebRTC/HLS/HTTP\-FLV/SRT.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]srs\-server\f[R] <\-h?vVgGe>|<[\-t] \-c filename>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SRS is a simple, high efficiency and realtime video server, supports
RTMP/WebRTC/HLS/HTTP\-FLV/SRT.
.PP
The options are as follows:
.PP
Options:
.PP
\-?, \-h, \[en]help : Show this help and exit 0.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\-v, \-V, \[en]version : Show version and exit 0.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\-g, \-G : Show server signature and exit 0.
\-e : Use environment variable only, ignore config file.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\-t : Test configuration file, exit with error code(0 for success).
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\-c filename : Use config file to start server.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
.SH FILES
.PP
/run/srs\-server.pid
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Contains the process ID of srs\-server.  The contents of this file are not sensitive, so it can be world\-readable.
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
/etc/srs\-server/srs.conf
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
The main configuration file.
    
\f[R]
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
Exit status is 0 on success, or 1 if the command fails.
.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
For example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
srs\-server \-v
srs\-server \-t \-c /etc/srs\-server/srs\-server.conf
srs\-server \-c /etc/srs\-server/srs\-server.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
Documentation at
https://ossrs.io/lts/en\-us/docs/v5/doc/getting\-started.
